    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios.jpg" alt="Philaretos Brachamios" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios">Philaretos Brachamios</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philaretos_Brachamios_Lead_Seal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Armenian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> was built by refugees. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas">Nicephorus II Phocas</a> retook Cilicia from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in 965 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> settled Christians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> there. More followed the Byzantine annexation of Greater Armenia in 1045 and its conquest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> nineteen years later: after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071 Malik-Shah's governors laid repressive taxes on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, who fled west. Some Armenian leaders set themselves up as lords; the most successful, the former Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios">Philaretos Brachamios</a>, held between 1078 and 1085 a principality from Malatia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and from Cilicia to Edessa; it crumbled before his death in 1090.</p><p>One of those nobles was Ruben, close to the last Bagratid king Gagik II, who was forced to cede his lands and assassinated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. In 1080, soon after, Ruben raised a band of Armenian troops and revolted against the Byzantine Empire, joined by many Armenian lords; the principality of Cilicia was founded under his leadership, and his descendants were the Rubenids. On his death in 1095 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> ruled from the family fortresses, contending with the Het'umids founded by the former Byzantine general Oshin. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> gave Constantine powerful allies: the Armenians supplied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> with horses, provisions and guidance, and with their help cleared the remaining Byzantine strongholds. Thoros I, who succeeded around 1100, moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> to Sis.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hromkla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hromkla.jpg" alt="Hromkla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hromkla">Hromkla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Euphrates_River_in_Turkey,_Rumkale_(52031477798).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato from Frankfurt, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cilician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> grew rich on its position at the junction of routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. It traded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, hides, wool and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, grain, wine and raw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. King Levon I made agreements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and with the French and Catalans, granting tax exemptions in return for their business, and European merchant colonies settled in the kingdom; Italian became the second language of commerce as French was of the nobility. The chief harbours were the fortified coastal sites of Ayas and Korikos and the river emporium of Mopsuestia on the Pyramus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from Ayas in 1271. From the thirteenth century the kingdom struck its own gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, the dram and tagvorin, at Sis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>.</p><p>The population was mixed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> formed the ruling class beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Muslims and various Europeans. Society moved from the traditional nakharar system towards Western feudalism; the titles baron and constable replaced nakharar and sparapet, nobles were knighted in the European manner, and the Armenian alphabet gained two letters, Ֆ and Օ, with Latin-based words. Ordinary Armenians were less won over, and most frowned on conversion to Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> or Greek Orthodoxy. The Church ran through the land: the Catholicos, assisted by fourteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> under Levon I, sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hromkla">Hromkla</a> from 1149 and at Sis after 1293, with up to sixty monasteries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/qalawun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qalawun.jpg" alt="Qalawun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%82%D8%A8%D8%A9_%D8%B6%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%AD_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%84%D8%B7%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%85%D9%84%D9%88%D9%83%D9%8A_%D9%82%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%88%D9%88%D9%86.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ahmed zakaria 2025, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom lived behind castles. Rubenid fortresses from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a> into the plain and the Het'umid castles of Lampron and Baberon guarded its approaches. Early wars were against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>: Thoros I took Cyzistra in 1112 and killed the Byzantine brothers who had murdered Gagik II; John II reconquered the Cilician plain in 1137 and carried Levon I and his sons to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, but Thoros II escaped in 1141 and held off the Byzantines until he paid homage to Manuel I in 1158.</p><p>From 1247 the Het'umids staked everything on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. Smbat's embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> brought back in 1250 a guarantee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>'s integrity; Het'um I visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> in 1253 and fought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1259–1260, where Arab historians charge his troops with a massacre and arson in the main mosque. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> answered. When Baibars summoned Het'um to change allegiance in 1266 and the king went to the Il-Khan for help, Al-Mansur Ali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a> overran the outnumbered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> at the Disaster of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, killing Prince T'oros, capturing Levon, sacking and burning Sis, massacring thousands and carrying off 40,000 captives. In 1275 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, burnt the palace, killed 15,000 civilians and took 10,000 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; almost all of Ayas's people perished. The truce of 1285 cost fortresses and a million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> a year, yet the raids went on, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hromkla">Hromkla</a> was sacked in 1292.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa.jpg" alt="Frederick Barbarossa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_I._Barbarossa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Montecappio, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For its first century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> was a principality whose rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> would not recognise as kings. Prince Levon II, who acceded in 1187, changed that: with the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperors</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> and Henry VI he was crowned in the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> on 6 January 1198, the Armenian Christmas, by the Catholicos Gregory VI Abirad. His designs on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> provoked the Antiochene Wars of Succession. In 1219 his daughter Zabel was proclaimed ruler under the regent Adam of Baghras; when he was assassinated the regency went to Constantine of Baberon, a Het'umid, who married her to Bohemond IV's son Philip. Philip, too <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for Armenian taste and accused of stealing the crown jewels, was imprisoned at Sis in 1224 and poisoned; Zabel was forced in 1226 to marry Constantine's son Het'um, who became co-ruler as Het'um I, uniting the two rival houses.</p><p>The Het'umids ruled through abdications and monasticism until Levon IV was killed by an angry mob in 1341 and the crown passed in 1342 to his cousin Guy de Lusignan as Constantine II. Religion was never settled: the Catholicos Grigor VI Apirat proclaimed union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1198 to no effect, and the Armenian delegate Mkhitar Skewratsi asked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1261 whence Rome drew “the power to pass judgment on the other Apostolic sees”. The Lusignans' drive to Latinise the Church was accepted by the nobles but resisted by the peasantry, and civil strife followed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/toros-roslin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toros-roslin.jpg" alt="Toros Roslin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/toros-roslin">Toros Roslin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roslin_statue_(Matenadaran).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Benoît Prieur, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> kept Armenian statehood alive while the Armenian highlands lay under foreign rule, and it became the focus of Armenian cultural production. The Catholicosate followed its people, settling at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hromkla">Hromkla</a> in 1149 and Sis in 1293; the illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toros-roslin">Toros Roslin</a>, working at Hromkla, are among the finest Armenian art. The kingdom saw itself as a bastion of Christendom in the East and was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>' closest ally: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii">Pope Gregory XIII</a> later wrote that no people came to the Crusaders' aid “with the same enthusiasm, joy and faith as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> did”. Intermarriage bound Armenian and Frankish houses, and Armenian castle-building, with its rocky heights, curved walls and round towers, passed through Armenian masons into Crusader castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krak-des-chevaliers">Krak des Chevaliers</a> and Marqab.</p><p>The Armeno-Mongol alliance Het'um I forged in 1247–1253 made Cilicia a partner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a>'s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and in Ghazan's campaigns of 1299–1303, and a target of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>. In return the West changed Armenian society: chivalry, French titles, names and language, feudal ranks and two new letters. After the fall, 30,000 wealthy Armenians moved to Lusignan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and merchant families founded or joined diaspora communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The Holy See of Cilicia survives at Antelias in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, and the lion, emblem of the Cilician state, stands on the coat of arms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/crusade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crusade.jpg" alt="Crusade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Combat_deuxi%C3%A8me_croisade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymus, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mongol alliance that had protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> failed it in the fourteenth century as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> disintegrated. In 1307 Bularghu, the Mongol representative in Cilicia and a recent convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, murdered King Levon III, the former king Het'um II and their party; Oshin drove him out and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a> Oljeitu executed him, but the kingdom had lost its ally. The Het'umids ruled an unstable country until an angry mob killed Levon IV in 1341. In 1342 his cousin Guy de Lusignan was anointed Constantine II, and he and his brother John set out to impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and European ways on a Church and peasantry that refused them, so civil strife divided the kingdom just as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> invaded again in 1343–1344.</p><p>Appeals to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and plans for new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> brought nothing. Sis fell to the Mamluks, and in 1375 the fortress of Gaban, where King Levon V had taken refuge, fell too, ending the kingdom. Levon V was given safe passage and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> to seek John I's help, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1393; in 1396 his title passed to his cousin James I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Turkic tribes settled Cilicia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s conquest followed. Thirty thousand wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> left for Cyprus, while humbler Armenians stayed through Turkic and Ottoman rule, and Cilicia remained one of the most Armenian regions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> until the massacre of Cilician Armenians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a> in 1909.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Founded in 1080 by Ruben, an Armenian lord in revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and made a kingdom when Levon I was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> in 1198, Cilician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>' ally and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>' partner, grew rich on the trade of Ayas, and was worn down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> invasions and religious strife until Sis and Gaban fell in 1375.</em></p>

